# CrowdSec optional-integration example — the decisions-only Security Engine (component `crowdsec`,
# crowdsec). Copy to a private path OUTSIDE the checkout, fill every empty value, keep it mode 0600 and
# pass it with `docker compose --env-file`.
#
# Empty is a deliberate value: every line below that the manifest demands is a `${VAR:?}` in
# components/control/crowdsec/compose.yaml, so an unfilled install fails at `docker compose config`
# rather than starting something unspecified. A line carrying a NON-empty value is the `${VAR:-default}`
# shape: the manifest boots without it, and the value shown is what it boots as.
#
# What this example does NOT do: it is not in scripts/check_foundation.py's EXAMPLE_MANIFESTS, so the
# plain `python -B scripts/check_foundation.py` run does not walk it (that list is a fixed tuple and
# widening it is not this card's edit). Gate it explicitly:
#   python -B scripts/check_foundation.py --compose examples/crowdsec/compose.yaml
# and see README.md for the one platform-side line pair that makes alerts actually arrive.

# --- Image -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The manifest sets `pull_policy: never`, so this value must already exist in the local daemon.
# Third-party image: repository@sha256:<64 lowercase hex>, resolved for linux/amd64. Pin the value
# components/control/crowdsec/versions.json names as `image` — it also records the debian-flavour
# digest (the one journald needs), the index digest, the registry each came from and the date read.
LO_CROWDSEC_IMAGE=

# --- Read-only inputs the manifest demands -----------------------------------------------------
# Absolute directory of acquisition YAML, one file per log source (`filenames:` + `labels.type`).
# Mounted read-only at /etc/crowdsec/acquis.d with create_host_path: false, so a directory nobody
# wrote is a boot failure and not an empty mount — an engine watching nothing must not start quietly.
# If you read journald instead of files, this still needs a valid (possibly comment-only) file inside,
# and README.md names the two extra moves the debian flavour plus a /run/log/journal bind require.
LO_CROWDSEC_ACQUIS_DIR=
# Absolute directory holding the log files the acquisition YAML names, mounted read-only at /logs.
# Which host paths those are belongs to the overlay, not to the product (overlay compatibility).
LO_CROWDSEC_LOG_DIR=
# Absolute path to the rendered profiles.yaml. Its `filters` are what decides which alerts notify at
# all — the shipped shape in this directory restricts them to the brute-force scenarios threat detection engine kept.
LO_CROWDSEC_PROFILES_FILE=
# Absolute path to the rendered HTTP notification plugin config: the intake URL and the bearer token in
# its `headers:` block. This file CARRIES A CREDENTIAL (the `crowdsec` producer row of the platform role
# file), so it lives at mode 0400 on the host, outside git, and is the one secret on this component that
# the credential-name rule in scripts/check_foundation.py cannot see: the gate polices LO_* environment
# values, and this one travels inside a mounted YAML.
LO_CROWDSEC_NOTIFICATIONS_FILE=

# --- Defaulted ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The hub collections to install, space-separated. The default is the image's own default collection,
# quoted from build/docker/README.md at the pinned tag. threat detection engine narrows detection to ssh/proxy brute
# force; the collection names that narrowing needs are NOT filled in here, because no hub catalogue was
# reachable from this checkout on 2026-09-09 (hub.crowdsec.net's API answered 404 to a GET) and a name
# written from memory installs a parser nobody has seen. versions.json keeps that as an UNVERIFIED line;
# README.md says what to read before you change this.
LO_CROWDSEC_COLLECTIONS=crowdsecurity/linux
# community blocklist dependency: the community blocklist is off by default and one line opts in. `true` here means
# DISABLE_ONLINE_API=true — no Central API channel, no shared signals, no imported blocklist. Opting in
# is a human act before it is a value change: enrolling this machine ID needs an account and a key
# (README.md "The one step nobody can do for you"), and versions.json records that the variable's
# documented effect is "disable online API registration for signal sharing" rather than "stops the
# blocklist", which is a row conformance.md still owes.
LO_CROWDSEC_CAPI_DISABLED=true
